1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,904
Hi everyone, I am Ujwal.

2
00:00:03,366 --> 00:00:05,199
Same language subtitling.

3
00:00:05,199 --> 00:00:08,793
Captions in the language you are already hearing,

4
00:00:08,793 --> 00:00:12,606
highlighted word by word, on ordinary entertainment.

5
00:00:13,466 --> 00:00:16,546
Twenty years on Indian national television,

6
00:00:16,546 --> 00:00:18,552
two hundred million viewers.

7
00:00:18,552 --> 00:00:22,421
Thirty-two percentage points more children became good

8
00:00:22,421 --> 00:00:22,994
readers.

9
00:00:23,799 --> 00:00:28,259
Amazon already ships this for books, as Immersion Reading.

10
00:00:28,259 --> 00:00:32,335
No television platform has ever shipped it for video.

11
00:00:32,335 --> 00:00:34,335
EveryWord is that product.

12
00:00:35,566 --> 00:00:39,065
Real story, real captions from our pipeline.

13
00:00:39,065 --> 00:00:41,768
Every word lights as it is spoken,

14
00:00:41,768 --> 00:00:44,710
so watching becomes reading practice.

15
00:00:48,499 --> 00:00:51,559
And this counts the words the reader followed.

16
00:00:51,559 --> 00:00:52,823
Not minutes played.

17
00:00:52,823 --> 00:00:56,083
It is the only number here that measures reading.

18
00:00:56,532 --> 00:00:59,100
One button to hear a line again.

19
00:00:59,766 --> 00:01:02,494
And here it is running on a Fire TV,

20
00:01:02,494 --> 00:01:06,662
sideloaded as an app and driven entirely by the remote.

21
00:01:06,662 --> 00:01:09,390
Five stories, chosen with the D-pad.

22
00:01:10,266 --> 00:01:14,206
Same renderer as the browser, rebuilt for ten feet away.

23
00:01:14,206 --> 00:01:17,725
A child watching television gets reading practice,

24
00:01:17,725 --> 00:01:19,554
and nobody signed them up.

25
00:01:21,299 --> 00:01:25,391
The television reports what was read to our own server,

26
00:01:25,391 --> 00:01:28,888
so an assistant can answer how far a child got.

27
00:01:28,888 --> 00:01:30,971
That number came off the TV.

28
00:01:32,232 --> 00:01:33,204
Two ways in.

29
00:01:33,204 --> 00:01:36,850
A human recording timed by Amazon Transcribe,

30
00:01:36,850 --> 00:01:40,010
or any text read aloud by Amazon Polly,

31
00:01:40,010 --> 00:01:43,008
which reports when it said each word.

32
00:01:43,399 --> 00:01:46,143
Those captions cannot contain a wrong word,

33
00:01:46,143 --> 00:01:49,525
because the words were known before they were spoken.

34
00:01:49,525 --> 00:01:51,631
Any book can become a read-along.

35
00:01:52,466 --> 00:01:56,058
We measured our part against gold word alignments,

36
00:01:56,058 --> 00:01:58,070
on speech we did not record.

37
00:01:58,070 --> 00:02:01,878
The highlight lands within thirty milliseconds of the

38
00:02:01,878 --> 00:02:02,740
spoken word.

39
00:02:02,740 --> 00:02:03,890
About one frame.

40
00:02:04,232 --> 00:02:06,644
And it never lights a word early.

41
00:02:06,644 --> 00:02:09,568
Zero out of seven hundred and sixty-six.

42
00:02:09,568 --> 00:02:13,443
A highlight that runs ahead teaches a child the wrong

43
00:02:13,443 --> 00:02:13,808
word.

44
00:02:14,632 --> 00:02:18,449
It is for children, so here is everything it keeps.

45
00:02:18,449 --> 00:02:21,892
Five fields, and no microphone anywhere in it.

46
00:02:21,892 --> 00:02:26,008
A product that cannot listen cannot leak what it heard.

47
00:02:27,266 --> 00:02:31,729
A hundred and thirty million American adults read below a

48
00:02:31,729 --> 00:02:33,138
sixth grade level.

49
00:02:33,138 --> 00:02:37,445
This technique costs four tenths of a cent per learner.

50
00:02:37,445 --> 00:02:41,830
The renderer nobody had shipped is on npm, MIT licensed.

51
00:02:41,830 --> 00:02:42,613
EveryWord.

52
00:02:42,613 --> 00:02:44,570
Watching becomes reading.

53
00:02:45,032 --> 00:02:46,208
Thank you.
